module nupp.io

--[[
`nupp.io` is bytes: storage for them, and the readers and writers that move
them. Reach for it when a program needs a growable buffer, an immutable
snapshot of one, or a parser that works the same over a buffer, a file and an
HTTP response body.

```nupp:playground
local bytes = nupp.io.newBuffer("hello")
local reader = bytes:newReader()
assert(reader:read(5) == "hello")
```

A buffer holds its bytes in a LuaJIT FFI array, so a program that uses one adds
no native dependency. `Reader` and `Writer` are interfaces rather than the
concrete things that satisfy them, which is what lets code written against the
contract work over any of them without knowing which it has.

The rest of what `io` means is a module of its own, because each has a luacase
name and so can have one. See [](nupp.io.path) for filesystem names,
[](nupp.io.uri) for resource identifiers, [](nupp.io.files) for the filesystem
itself, [](nupp.io.process) for running a child process, [](nupp.io.net) for
network connections, [](nupp.io.tls) for encrypting one, and [](nupp.io.http)
for the asynchronous HTTP client.
Keeping them apart is what lets a program that wants a byte buffer carry a byte
buffer rather than every provider behind the name.

The last four move their bytes through the `Reader` and `Writer` contracts on
this page, so a parser written against byte I/O reads a file, a child's output,
a connection, or a response body without knowing which it has.

Every value here is an owner. `close` is safe to call repeatedly and answers
whether the thing it was writing into was still open, so a caller that cares can
tell a clean finish from a destination that went away first. `isReleased`
reports that state without changing it.

## Buffers

`newBuffer()` creates an empty growable buffer. A string supplies initial
bytes; an integer reserves capacity without changing the length.

```nupp
local bytes = nupp.io.newBuffer("hello")
bytes:setString("!", bytes:length())
assert(bytes:getString() == "hello!")
```

Buffer offsets are zero-based, as they are wherever a count is an offset into
storage rather than a position in a Lua string. See [Byte
positions](docs/concepts/standard-library.md#byte-positions) for the rule and
where the other convention applies.

`getString(offset, count)` copies a range. `setString` overwrites from an
offset and grows the buffer as needed, filling any gap with zero bytes. `clear`
sets the length to zero without discarding capacity, and `resize` truncates or
zero-fills:

```nupp
local bytes = nupp.io.newBuffer("hello")
bytes:ensureCapacity(4096)
assert(bytes:capacity() >= 4096)
bytes:resize(3)
assert(bytes:getString() == "hel")
```

Capacity is the allocation, not a recorded number. Growing at least doubles it,
so appending through a writer costs amortized constant time per byte and
`capacity()` reports bytes that are actually held. `ensureCapacity` reserves at
least the minimum asked for.

`close()` releases the buffer's storage, and an operation on a released buffer
raises rather than answering a reason.

`readSpan()` borrows the buffer's initialized bytes without separating its
pointer from its length. Native producers write through `reserveWrite(offset,
count)`: the returned lease supplies a checked writable span, and
`commit(written)` changes the logical length only after the producer succeeds.
Dropping the lease aborts it and leaves the length unchanged.

## Byte views

`view(offset, count)` returns an immutable snapshot, not a mutable alias into
the buffer. It remains valid if the source buffer changes or closes:

```nupp
local buffer = nupp.io.newBuffer("header-body")
local header = buffer:view(0, 6)
buffer:clear()
assert(header:getString() == "header")
header:close()
```

A view can make a smaller view, open its own snapshot reader, report its byte
length, copy to a string, borrow a checked span with `readSpan()`, and be
closed. Its offsets are zero-based like a buffer's. The data and UTF-8 modules
accept views, which is what keeps those APIs from being coupled to a mutable
buffer.

## Readers

A `nupp.io.Reader` is a forward-only byte source. `newStringReader(text)` reads
a string; `buffer:newReader()` reads a snapshot of the buffer's current
contents. A reader owns its close obligation and closes automatically at the
end of its scope unless `close()` consumes it earlier.

```nupp
local reader = nupp.io.newStringReader("abcdef")
assert(reader:read(2) == "ab")

local destination = nupp.io.newBuffer()
assert(reader:readInto(destination, 0, 3) == 3)
assert(destination:getString() == "cde")
assert(reader:read(8) == "f")
assert(reader:read(8) == "") -- EOF
```

`read(count)` requires a positive count and returns at most that many bytes. It
returns an empty string at EOF and nil with a reason after close.
`readSpan(writable)` fills a positive-sized checked writable span.
`readInto(buffer, offset, count)` returns zero at EOF and reads 64 KiB when
given no count; an explicit count must be positive. A failed or EOF read does
not extend the destination. `transferTo(writer)` copies the entire remaining
source and returns the byte count.

## Writers

`buffer:newWriter()` clears the buffer and returns a forward-only writer
targeting it. A writer likewise closes automatically; `flush()` publishes
pending work without consuming that ownership.

```nupp
local destination = nupp.io.newBuffer()
local writer = destination:newWriter()
assert(writer:write("prefix:"))

local payload = nupp.io.newBuffer("body")
assert(writer:writeSpan(payload:readSpan()) == 4)
assert(writer:flush())
assert(destination:getString() == "prefix:body")
```

`write` answers a boolean and `writeSpan` answers the byte count. Both answer a
reason when the writer is closed or the destination was released. The span is
the common zero-copy input contract for buffers, views, files, processes, and
HTTP transfers; there is no unchecked raw-pointer writer alongside it. `flush`
does nothing for memory and is part of the contract so that a later file or
socket writer implements the same interface.

## Typed scalars

A reader and a writer move bytes. `newScalarReader` and `newScalarWriter` add
the missing piece, a sized integer or float landing on those bytes without an
`ffi.cast` at every call site.

```nupp
local writer = nupp.io.newScalarWriter()
writer:writeUint32(1447383632 as uint32):writeFloat64(1.5)

local stored = writer:buffer()
assert(stored ~= nil)
local reader = nupp.io.newScalarReader(stored as nupp.io.Buffer)
assert(reader:readUint32() == (1447383632 as uint32))
assert(reader:readFloat64() == 1.5)
assert(reader:atEnd())
```

Every write answers the writer, so calls chain. A short read raises rather than
answering a reason, which is the one place this pair departs from the reader
and writer contracts above: a scalar either landed whole or the source was not
what the format said, and there is no partial value to hand back.

::: deepdive Host byte order
Both directions read and write host-endian, the only order LuaJIT ships, so
that is the default. A format fixed to one byte order casts and swaps
explicitly, and there are no LE and BE variants here until something needs
them. Adding a second set without a caller would double the surface and leave
half of it untested.
:::

### Scalar sources

`newScalarReader` takes whatever holds the bytes. A string, a `ByteView` or a
`Buffer` is read from a copy taken there and then, so `remaining()` knows the
count:

```nupp
local reader = nupp.io.newScalarReader("\1\2\3\4")
assert(reader:remaining() == 4)
```

A `Reader` is consumed as it goes, which is what lets a file or an HTTP body be
read a field at a time. It cannot say how much is left, so `remaining()`
answers nil and `atEnd()` is the question to ask instead. The scalar adapter
takes that reader: closing or dropping the adapter closes or drops the reader
exactly once.

### Scalar destinations

`newScalarWriter` appends to a `Buffer`, keeping what that buffer already
holds, or writes through a `Writer`. Given nothing it starts a buffer of its
own, which `buffer()` hands back:

```nupp
local destination = nupp.io.newBuffer("header:")
nupp.io.newScalarWriter(destination):writeUint8(33 as uint32)
assert(destination:getString() == "header:!")
```

A writer pointed at somebody else's `Writer` answers nil from `buffer()`, since
the bytes are already gone. That writer is taken, so closing or dropping the
scalar adapter closes or drops it exactly once. A buffer supplied by the caller
is only borrowed. The buffer created by the no-argument form belongs to the
scalar writer; `buffer()` borrows it for as long as the scalar writer remains
live.

## Byte queues

A `string.buffer` is a byte queue: `put` appends to the back and `get` consumes
from the front. `newScalarReader` accepts one directly, so bytes assembled
there are read without copying everything in it first, and the queue's own
`get` stays usable over the same bytes.

```nupp
local buffer = require("string.buffer")

local queue = buffer.new()
queue:put("header:")
queue:put("!")

local reader = nupp.io.newScalarReader(queue)
assert(reader:readBytes(7) == "header:")
assert(queue:tostring() == "!")
assert(reader:readUint8() == (33 as uint8))
```

Reading drains the queue, and `remaining()` reports what it still holds.
`newQueueReader` is the same bridge one layer down: it answers an ordinary
`Reader` over the queue, for `transferTo`, `readInto`, and everything else that
contract already covers.

```nupp
local buffer = require("string.buffer")

local destination = nupp.io.newBuffer()
local queue = buffer.new()
queue:put("payload")
assert(nupp.io.newQueueReader(queue):transferTo(destination:newWriter()) == 7)
assert(destination:getString() == "payload")
```

Neither takes the queue over. Nothing here frees or closes it, and one read to
empty leaves an empty queue rather than a released one.

::: seealso
- [](nupp.io.files) for the same contracts over a file on disk
- [](nupp.io.path) for filesystem names rather than file contents
- [ownership.md](docs/type-system/ownership.md) for the complete contract every
  value here is written against
- [standard-library.md](docs/concepts/standard-library.md) for the errors,
  ownership and byte-position conventions every facility shares
:::
]]

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Argument checks
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

local ffi = require("ffi")
local span = require("nupp.mem.span")

local SMALLEST = 32
local DEFAULT_READ = 65536

--- @raises when value is not a non-negative integer
local function integer_(value: any, what: string, level: integer): integer
    if type(value) ~= "number" or value ~= math.floor(value as number) or (value as number) < 0 then
        error("nupp: " .. what .. " must be a non-negative integer", level)
    end

    return value as integer
end

--- @raises when value is not an integer
local function whole(value: any, what: string, level: integer): integer
    if type(value) ~= "number" or value ~= math.floor(value as number) then
        error("nupp: " .. what .. " must be an integer", level)
    end

    return value as integer
end

--- @raises when value is not a positive integer
local function positive(value: any, what: string, level: integer): integer
    local answer = whole(value, what, level + 1)
    if answer < 1 then
        error("nupp: " .. what .. " must be a positive integer", level)
    end

    return answer
end

--- @raises when the owner is closed
local function opened(closed: boolean, what: string, level: integer): nil
    if closed then
        error("nupp: " .. what .. " is closed", level)
    end
end

-- An omitted offset is the start and an omitted count is the rest, so a caller that
-- wants everything says nothing.

--- @raises when the range is not within length
local function range(length: integer, offset: any, count: any, what: string, level: integer): (integer, integer)
    local from = integer_(offset or 0, what .. " offset", level)
    if from > length then
        error("nupp: " .. what .. " offset is past the end", level)
    end
    local taking = integer_(count == nil and length - from or count, what .. " count", level)
    if from + taking > length then
        error("nupp: " .. what .. " range is past the end", level)
    end

    return from, taking
end

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Owners
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- A terminal has to be a declared function wherever a contract names it, and these
-- records hand each other out: a buffer answers a reader, and a snapshot answers
-- another snapshot. Written one per record, each terminal would have to follow the
-- record it takes and precede the record that hands it out, and no such order exists.
-- One generic terminal over the shape they all share is written once, ahead of them.
local interface Owner
    drop: nosuspend function(takes self: Owner): nil
end

--- Releases any of this module's owners. Every one of them is handed out under this
--- exact cleanup, which is why a caller never names it.
local function destroyOwner<T is Owner>(takes self: T): nil
    self:drop()
end

--- An appending byte sink.
---
--- The counterpart of `Reader`, and an interface for the same reason: a buffer,
--- an open file and a request body all satisfy it, and a producer written
--- against the contract fills any of them.
--- @export
export affine interface Writer is nupp.Closeable

    --- Appends bytes.
    --- @param self this writer
    --- @param bytes the bytes to append
    --- @return whether they were written
    --- @return why they were not, when unsuccessful
    --- @raises when bytes is not a string
    write: function(exclusive self: Writer, bytes: string): (boolean, string?)

    --- Appends bytes from a checked shared span.
    --- @param self this writer
    --- @param source bytes valid for the duration of the call
    --- @return how many bytes moved, or nil on failure
    --- @return why it could not, when unsuccessful
    writeSpan: function(exclusive self: Writer, borrows source: span.ByteSpan): (integer?, string?)

    --- Nothing is buffered behind this writer, so this only reports whether it is open.
    --- @param self this writer
    --- @return whether the writer is open
    --- @return why it is not, when it is not
    flush: function(self: Writer): (boolean, string?)

end

--- A forward-only byte source.
---
--- An interface rather than the concrete things that satisfy it. A buffer's
--- reader, a file's reader and an HTTP response body are all one of these, so
--- code written against the contract works over any of them without knowing
--- which it has.
--- @export
export affine interface Reader is nupp.Closeable

    --- Reads up to `count` bytes.
    ---
    --- An empty answer is the end.
    --- @param self this reader
    --- @param count the most bytes to read
    --- @return the bytes, or nil when the reader is closed
    --- @return why it could not read, when unsuccessful
    --- @raises when count is not a positive integer
    read: function(self: Reader, count: integer): (string?, string?)

    --- Reads directly into a checked writable span. A zero answer is the end.
    --- @param self this reader
    --- @param destination the positive-sized range to fill
    --- @return how many bytes were read, or nil when the reader is closed
    --- @return why it could not read, when unsuccessful
    readSpan: function(self: Reader, exclusive destination: span.Writable<uint8>): (integer?, string?)

    --- Reads into a buffer.
    ---
    --- A zero answer is the end.
    --- @param self this reader
    --- @param destination the buffer to write into
    --- @param offset where in the destination to start, or the beginning
    --- @param count the most bytes to read
    --- @return how many bytes were read, or nil when the reader is closed
    --- @return why it could not read, when unsuccessful
    readInto: function(
        self: Reader,
        exclusive destination: Buffer,
        offset: integer?,
        count: integer?
    ): (integer?, string?)

    --- Writes everything left to a writer.
    --- @param self this reader
    --- @param destination the writer to fill
    --- @return how many bytes moved, or nil on failure
    --- @return why it could not, when unsuccessful
    transferTo: function(self: Reader, exclusive destination: Writer): (integer?, string?)

end

-- Declared ahead of the records because their methods call them, and defined below
-- because they name the records. The types are the contract either way.
local reserve: function(borrows self: Buffer, minimum: integer): nil
local bytesAt: function(borrows self: Buffer, offset: integer, count: integer): string
local newView: function(bytes: string): affine(ByteView, destroyOwner)
local newReaderOver: function(bytes: string): Reader
local newWriterOver: function(borrows buffer: Buffer): affine(Writer) borrows (buffer)
local fill: function(self: ScalarReader, need: integer): (integer, string?)
local taken: function(self: ScalarReader, need: integer): string
local put: function(self: ScalarWriter, bytes: string): ScalarWriter

--- A queue of bytes something else is filling, which a reader may be opened over
--- without owning it.
---
--- A process stream and an HTTP response body are both one of these without either
--- knowing about this module.
--- @export
export interface ByteQueue
    metamethod __len: function(self): integer

    --- Consumes bytes from the front and returns them, or all of them when given no
    --- count.
    --- @param self this queue
    --- @param ... how many bytes each returned string takes
    --- @return the consumed bytes
    get: function(self: ByteQueue, ...: integer?): string
end

--- A snapshot of bytes that cannot be written through.
--- @export
export sealed interface ByteView
    drop: nosuspend function(takes self: ByteView): nil
    length: function(borrows self: ByteView): integer
    getString: function(borrows self: ByteView): string
    readSpan: function(borrows self: ByteView): span.ByteSpan borrows (self)
    newReader: function(self: ByteView): Reader
    view: function(self: ByteView, offset: integer?, count: integer?): affine(ByteView, destroyOwner)
    isReleased: function(self: ByteView): boolean
    close: function(takes self: ByteView): (boolean, string?)
end

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Views and buffers
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

local record Snapshot is ByteView
    private _bytes: string
    private _closed: boolean

    drop: nosuspend function(takes self: Snapshot): nil

    function length(borrows self): integer
        opened(self._closed, "io.ByteView", 2)
        return #self._bytes
    end

    function getString(borrows self): string
        opened(self._closed, "io.ByteView", 2)
        return self._bytes
    end

    function readSpan(borrows self): span.ByteSpan borrows (self)
        opened(self._closed, "io.ByteView", 2)
        return span.fromString(self._bytes)
    end

    function newReader(self): Reader
        opened(self._closed, "io.ByteView", 2)
        return newReaderOver(self._bytes)
    end

    function view(self, offset: integer?, count: integer?): affine(ByteView, destroyOwner)
        opened(self._closed, "io.ByteView", 2)
        local from, taking = range(#self._bytes, offset, count, "io.ByteView", 2)
        return newView(self._bytes:sub(from + 1, from + taking))
    end

    function isReleased(self): boolean
        return self._closed
    end

    function close(takes self): (boolean, string?)
        self:drop()
        return true, nil
    end
end

--- An exclusive prepared region of a buffer. Its span may be written, then the
--- lease is committed with the number of bytes that actually landed. Dropping a
--- lease aborts it without changing the buffer's logical length.
--- @export
export sealed interface BufferWriteLease
    drop: nosuspend function(takes self: BufferWriteLease): nil
    span: function(exclusive self: BufferWriteLease): span.Writable<uint8> borrows (self)
    commit: function(takes self: BufferWriteLease, written: integer): nil
end

--- Growable byte storage whose pointer and logical length never escape separately.
--- @export
export sealed interface Buffer
    drop: nosuspend function(takes self: Buffer): nil
    length: function(borrows self: Buffer): integer
    capacity: function(borrows self: Buffer): integer
    clear: function(exclusive self: Buffer): nil
    ensureCapacity: function(exclusive self: Buffer, minimum: integer): nil
    resize: function(exclusive self: Buffer, length: integer): nil
    getString: function(borrows self: Buffer, offset: integer?, count: integer?): string
    setString: function(exclusive self: Buffer, bytes: string, offset: integer?): nil
    readSpan: function(borrows self: Buffer): span.ByteSpan borrows (self)
    reserveWrite: function(
        exclusive self: Buffer,
        offset: integer,
        count: integer
    ): affine(BufferWriteLease, destroyOwner) borrows (self)
    view: function(self: Buffer, offset: integer?, count: integer?): affine(ByteView, destroyOwner)
    newReader: function(self: Buffer): Reader
    newWriter: function(exclusive self: Buffer): affine(Writer) borrows (self)
    isReleased: function(borrows self: Buffer): boolean
    close: function(takes self: Buffer): (boolean, string?)
end

local newWriteLease: function(
    borrows buffer: Buffer,
    offset: integer,
    count: integer
): affine(BufferWriteLease, destroyOwner) borrows (buffer)

local record HeapBuffer is Buffer
    private _data: uint8[?]
    private _length: integer
    private _capacity: integer
    private _closed: boolean

    drop: nosuspend function(takes self: HeapBuffer): nil

    function length(borrows self): integer
        opened(self._closed, "io.Buffer", 2)
        return self._length
    end

    function capacity(borrows self): integer
        opened(self._closed, "io.Buffer", 2)
        return self._capacity
    end

    function clear(exclusive self): nil
        opened(self._closed, "io.Buffer", 2)
        self._length = 0
    end

    function ensureCapacity(exclusive self, minimum: integer): nil
        opened(self._closed, "io.Buffer", 2)
        reserve(self, integer_(minimum, "io.Buffer capacity", 2))
    end

    function resize(exclusive self, length: integer): nil
        opened(self._closed, "io.Buffer", 2)
        local wanted = integer_(length, "io.Buffer length", 2)
        if wanted > self._length then
            reserve(self, wanted)
            unsafe do
                local root = borrowFrom(self._data as voidptr, self)
                ffi.fill((root as uint8*) + self._length, wanted - self._length, 0)
            end
        end
        self._length = wanted
    end

    function getString(borrows self, offset: integer?, count: integer?): string
        opened(self._closed, "io.Buffer", 2)
        local from, taking = range(self._length, offset, count, "io.Buffer", 2)
        return bytesAt(self, from, taking)
    end

    function setString(exclusive self, bytes: string, offset: integer?): nil
        opened(self._closed, "io.Buffer", 2)
        if type(bytes) ~= "string" then
            error("nupp: io.Buffer bytes must be a string", 2)
        end
        local from = integer_(offset or 0, "io.Buffer offset", 2)
        local ending = from + #bytes
        reserve(self, ending)
        if from > self._length then
            unsafe do
                local root = borrowFrom(self._data as voidptr, self)
                ffi.fill((root as uint8*) + self._length, from - self._length, 0)
            end
        end
        if #bytes > 0 then
            unsafe do
                local root = borrowFrom(self._data as voidptr, self)
                ffi.copy((root as uint8*) + from, bytes, #bytes)
            end
        end
        if ending > self._length then
            self._length = ending
        end
    end

    function readSpan(borrows self): span.ByteSpan borrows (self)
        opened(self._closed, "io.Buffer", 2)
        return span.fromCarray(self._data, self._length)
    end

    function reserveWrite(
        exclusive self,
        offset: integer,
        count: integer
    ): affine(BufferWriteLease, destroyOwner) borrows (self)
        opened(self._closed, "io.Buffer", 2)
        local from = integer_(offset, "io.Buffer write offset", 2)
        local available = integer_(count, "io.Buffer write count", 2)
        reserve(self, from + available)

        return newWriteLease(self, from, available)
    end

    function view(self, offset: integer?, count: integer?): affine(ByteView, destroyOwner)
        opened(self._closed, "io.Buffer", 2)
        local from, taking = range(self._length, offset, count, "io.Buffer", 2)
        return newView(bytesAt(self, from, taking))
    end

    function newReader(self): Reader
        opened(self._closed, "io.Buffer", 2)
        return newReaderOver(bytesAt(self, 0, self._length))
    end

    function newWriter(exclusive self): affine(Writer) borrows (self)
        opened(self._closed, "io.Buffer", 2)
        self:clear()
        return newWriterOver(self)
    end

    function isReleased(borrows self): boolean
        return self._closed
    end

    function close(takes self): (boolean, string?)
        self:drop()
        return true, nil
    end
end

local record WriteLease is BufferWriteLease
    private readonly anchor: any
    private readonly state: any
    private readonly pointer: uint8[?]
    private readonly offset: integer
    private readonly count: integer

    drop: nosuspend function(takes self: WriteLease): nil

    function span(exclusive self): span.Writable<uint8> borrows (self)
        return span.writeCarray(self.pointer, self.count)
    end

    function commit(takes self, written: integer): nil
        local used = integer_(written, "io.Buffer committed byte count", 2)
        if used > self.count then
            error("nupp: io.Buffer committed byte count exceeds its lease", 2)
        end
        local raw = unsafe release self
        local target = raw.state
        opened(target._closed, "io.Buffer", 2)
        if used > 0 then
            if raw.offset > target._length then
                unsafe do
                    local root = borrowFrom(target._data as voidptr, target)
                    ffi.fill(
                        (root as uint8*) + (target._length as integer),
                        (raw.offset as integer) - (target._length as integer),
                        0
                    )
                end
            end
            local ending = raw.offset + used
            if ending > target._length then
                target._length = ending
            end
        end
    end
end

newWriteLease = function(
    borrows buffer: Buffer,
    offset: integer,
    count: integer
): affine(BufferWriteLease, destroyOwner) borrows (buffer)
    local state = buffer as any
    return new WriteLease(
        anchor = buffer,
        state = state,
        pointer = (state._data + offset) as uint8[?],
        offset = offset,
        count = count
    )
end

--- The storage behind a writer over a buffer.
local record BufferWriter is Writer
    private readonly anchor: any
    private readonly _buffer: any
    private _at: integer
    private _closed: boolean

    --- Appends bytes.
    --- @param self this writer
    --- @param bytes the bytes to append
    --- @return whether they were written
    --- @return why they were not, when unsuccessful
    --- @raises when bytes is not a string
    function write(exclusive self, bytes: string): (boolean, string?)
        if self._closed then
            return false, "the writer is closed"
        end
        if type(bytes) ~= "string" then
            error("nupp: Writer:write bytes must be a string", 2)
        end
        local source = span.fromString(bytes)
        local written, reason = self:writeSpan(source)
        if written == nil then
            return false, reason
        end

        return true, nil
    end

    function writeSpan(exclusive self, borrows source: span.ByteSpan): (integer?, string?)
        if self._closed then
            return nil, "the writer is closed"
        end
        local state = self._buffer
        if state._closed then
            return nil, "the destination buffer is closed"
        end
        local count = #source
        if count == 0 then
            return 0, nil
        end
        local at = self._at
        reserve(state as Buffer, at + count)
        local lease = newWriteLease(state as Buffer, at, count)
        do
            local output = lease:span()
            for index = 1, count do
                output[index] = source[index]
            end
            drop output
        end
        lease:commit(count)
        self._at = at + count

        return count, nil
    end

    --- Nothing is buffered behind this writer, so this only reports whether it is open.
    --- @param self this writer
    --- @return whether the writer is open
    --- @return why it is not, when it is not
    function flush(self): (boolean, string?)
        if self._closed then
            return false, "the writer is closed"
        end

        return true, nil
    end

    --- Closes the writer.
    --- @param self this writer, spent by the call
    function close(takes self): nil
        self:drop()
    end
end

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Readers
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

--- The storage behind a reader over held bytes.
local record StringReader is Reader
    _bytes: string
    _at: integer
    _closed: boolean

    --- Reads up to `count` bytes.
    ---
    --- An empty answer is the end.
    --- @param self this reader
    --- @param count the most bytes to read
    --- @return the bytes, or nil when the reader is closed
    --- @return why it could not read, when unsuccessful
    --- @raises when count is not a positive integer
    function read(self, count: integer): (string?, string?)
        if self._closed then
            return nil, "the reader is closed"
        end
        local wanted = positive(count, "Reader:read count", 2)
        local bytes = self._bytes
        local at = self._at
        if at > #bytes then
            return "", nil
        end
        local taking = math.min(wanted, #bytes - at + 1)
        local out = bytes:sub(at, at + (taking as integer) - 1)
        self._at = at + (taking as integer)

        return out, nil
    end

    function readSpan(self, exclusive destination: span.Writable<uint8>): (integer?, string?)
        if self._closed then
            return nil, "the reader is closed"
        end
        local wanted = positive(#destination, "Reader:readSpan destination length", 2)
        local bytes = self._bytes
        local at = self._at
        if at > #bytes then
            return 0, nil
        end
        local taking = math.min(wanted, #bytes - at + 1) as integer
        local chunk = bytes:sub(at, at + taking - 1)
        local input = span.fromString(chunk)
        for index = 1, taking do
            destination[index] = input[index]
        end
        self._at = at + taking

        return taking, nil
    end

    function readInto(self, exclusive destination: Buffer, offset: integer?, count: integer?): (integer?, string?)
        if self._closed then
            return nil, "the reader is closed"
        end
        local from = integer_(offset or 0, "Reader:readInto offset", 2)
        local wanted = positive(count or DEFAULT_READ, "Reader:readInto count", 2)
        local bytes = self._bytes
        local at = self._at
        if at > #bytes then
            return 0, nil
        end
        local taking = math.min(wanted, #bytes - at + 1) as integer
        local chunk = bytes:sub(at, at + taking - 1)
        local input = span.fromString(chunk)
        local lease = destination:reserveWrite(from, wanted)
        do
            local output = lease:span()
            for index = 1, taking do
                output[index] = input[index]
            end
            drop output
        end
        lease:commit(taking)
        self._at = at + taking

        return taking, nil
    end

    --- Writes everything left to a writer.
    --- @param self this reader
    --- @param destination the writer to fill
    --- @return how many bytes moved, or nil on failure
    --- @return why it could not, when unsuccessful
    function transferTo(self, exclusive destination: Writer): (integer?, string?)
        if self._closed then
            return nil, "the reader is closed"
        end
        local remaining = self._bytes:sub(self._at)
        local ok, reason = destination:write(remaining)
        if not ok then
            return nil, reason
        end
        self._at = #self._bytes + 1

        return #remaining, nil
    end

    --- Closes the reader.
    ---
    --- Repeated calls are safe.
    --- @param self this reader, spent by the call
    function close(takes self): nil
        self:drop()
    end
end

--- A reader over anything answering `get`.
--- @export
local record QueueReader is Reader
    _source: any
    _closed: boolean

    --- Reads up to `count` bytes from the queue.
    --- @param self this reader
    --- @param count the most bytes to read
    --- @return the bytes, or nil when the reader is closed
    --- @return why it could not read, when unsuccessful
    --- @raises when count is not a positive integer
    function read(self, count: integer): (string?, string?)
        if self._closed then
            return nil, "the reader is closed"
        end
        local wanted = positive(count, "Reader:read count", 2)
        local source = self._source
        local have = #source
        if have == 0 then
            return "", nil
        end

        return source:get(math.min(wanted, have)), nil
    end

    function readSpan(self, exclusive destination: span.Writable<uint8>): (integer?, string?)
        if self._closed then
            return nil, "the reader is closed"
        end
        local wanted = positive(#destination, "Reader:readSpan destination length", 2)
        local source = self._source
        local have = #source
        if have == 0 then
            return 0, nil
        end
        local taking = math.min(wanted, have) as integer
        local input = span.fromString(source:get(taking))
        for index = 1, taking do
            destination[index] = input[index]
        end

        return taking, nil
    end

    function readInto(self, exclusive destination: Buffer, offset: integer?, count: integer?): (integer?, string?)
        if self._closed then
            return nil, "the reader is closed"
        end
        local from = integer_(offset or 0, "Reader:readInto offset", 2)
        local wanted = positive(count or DEFAULT_READ, "Reader:readInto count", 2)
        local source = self._source
        local have = #source
        if have == 0 then
            return 0, nil
        end
        local taking = math.min(wanted, have) as integer
        local chunk = source:get(taking) as string
        local input = span.fromString(chunk)
        local lease = destination:reserveWrite(from, wanted)
        do
            local output = lease:span()
            for index = 1, taking do
                output[index] = input[index]
            end
            drop output
        end
        lease:commit(taking)

        return taking, nil
    end

    --- Writes everything the queue has to a writer.
    --- @param self this reader
    --- @param destination the writer to fill
    --- @return how many bytes moved, or nil on failure
    --- @return why it could not, when unsuccessful
    function transferTo(self, exclusive destination: Writer): (integer?, string?)
        if self._closed then
            return nil, "the reader is closed"
        end
        local rest = self._source:get() as string
        local ok, reason = destination:write(rest)
        if not ok then
            return nil, reason
        end

        return #rest, nil
    end

    --- Releases it as an ownership terminal.
    ---
    --- Repeated calls are safe. Declared rather than written inline: a cleanup
    --- contract is `nosuspend`, and an inline method cannot carry one.
    --- @param self this reader, spent by the call
    drop: nosuspend function(takes self: QueueReader): nil

    --- Closes the reader without closing the queue behind it.
    --- @param self this reader, spent by the call
    function close(takes self): nil
        self:drop()
    end
end

--- Reads fixed-width numbers from a source of bytes.
--- @export
export interface ScalarReader
    _pending: string
    _reader: any
    _queue: any
    _closed: boolean

    --- Releases it as an ownership terminal.
    ---
    --- Repeated calls are safe. Declared rather than written inline: a cleanup
    --- contract is `nosuspend`, and an inline method cannot carry one.
    --- @param self this owner, spent by the call
    drop: nosuspend function(takes self: ScalarReader): nil

    --- How many bytes are left, when that is knowable.
    --- @param self this reader
    --- @return the remaining byte count, or nil over an open-ended source
    --- @raises when the reader is closed
    function remaining(self): integer?
        if self._closed then
            error("nupp: io.ScalarReader is closed", 2)
        end
        if self._reader then
            return nil
        end
        if self._queue then
            return #self._pending + #self._queue
        end

        return #self._pending
    end

    --- Whether the source has nothing left.
    --- @param self this reader
    --- @return whether it is at the end
    --- @raises when the reader is closed, or its source failed
    function atEnd(self): boolean
        if self._closed then
            error("nupp: io.ScalarReader is closed", 2)
        end
        local have, failure = fill(self, 1)
        if failure then
            error("nupp: io.ScalarReader source failed: " .. tostring(failure), 2)
        end

        return have < 1
    end

    --- Discards bytes.
    --- @param self this reader
    --- @param count how many to discard
    --- @return this reader
    --- @raises when there are not that many
    function skip(self, count: integer): ScalarReader
        taken(self, integer_(count, "io.ScalarReader count", 2))

        return self
    end

    --- Reads bytes as they are.
    --- @param self this reader
    --- @param count how many to read
    --- @return the bytes
    --- @raises when there are not that many
    function readBytes(self, count: integer): string
        return taken(self, integer_(count, "io.ScalarReader count", 2))
    end

    --- Reads one unsigned 8-bit integer.
    --- @param self this reader
    --- @return the value, 0 through 255
    --- @raises when the source has no bytes left
    function readUint8(self): uint32
        local raw = taken(self, 1)
        local holder = ffi.new<uint8[1]>()
        local value: any = nil
        -- A pointer is not indexable in checked code, so the bytes are copied into a
        -- one-element array and read from there. Same instruction, checked shape.
        unsafe do
            ffi.copy(holder, raw, 1)
            value = holder[0]
        end

        return nupp.math.u32.wrap(value as integer)
    end

    --- Reads one signed 8-bit integer.
    --- @param self this reader
    --- @return the value, -128 through 127
    --- @raises when the source has no bytes left
    function readInt8(self): int32
        local raw = taken(self, 1)
        local holder = ffi.new<int8[1]>()
        local value: any = nil
        -- A pointer is not indexable in checked code, so the bytes are copied into a
        -- one-element array and read from there. Same instruction, checked shape.
        unsafe do
            ffi.copy(holder, raw, 1)
            value = holder[0]
        end

        return nupp.math.i32.wrap(value as integer)
    end

    --- Reads one unsigned 16-bit integer in the machine's byte order.
    --- @param self this reader
    --- @return the value, 0 through 65535
    --- @raises when fewer than 2 bytes are left
    function readUint16(self): uint32
        local raw = taken(self, 2)
        local holder = ffi.new<uint16[1]>()
        local value: any = nil
        -- A pointer is not indexable in checked code, so the bytes are copied into a
        -- one-element array and read from there. Same instruction, checked shape.
        unsafe do
            ffi.copy(holder, raw, 2)
            value = holder[0]
        end

        return nupp.math.u32.wrap(value as integer)
    end

    --- Reads one signed 16-bit integer in the machine's byte order.
    --- @param self this reader
    --- @return the value, -32768 through 32767
    --- @raises when fewer than 2 bytes are left
    function readInt16(self): int32
        local raw = taken(self, 2)
        local holder = ffi.new<int16[1]>()
        local value: any = nil
        -- A pointer is not indexable in checked code, so the bytes are copied into a
        -- one-element array and read from there. Same instruction, checked shape.
        unsafe do
            ffi.copy(holder, raw, 2)
            value = holder[0]
        end

        return nupp.math.i32.wrap(value as integer)
    end

    --- Reads one unsigned 32-bit integer in the machine's byte order.
    --- @param self this reader
    --- @return the value, 0 through 4294967295
    --- @raises when fewer than 4 bytes are left
    function readUint32(self): uint32
        local raw = taken(self, 4)
        local holder = ffi.new<uint32[1]>()
        local value: any = nil
        -- A pointer is not indexable in checked code, so the bytes are copied into a
        -- one-element array and read from there. Same instruction, checked shape.
        unsafe do
            ffi.copy(holder, raw, 4)
            value = holder[0]
        end

        return nupp.math.u32.wrap(value as integer)
    end

    --- Reads one signed 32-bit integer in the machine's byte order.
    --- @param self this reader
    --- @return the value
    --- @raises when fewer than 4 bytes are left
    function readInt32(self): int32
        local raw = taken(self, 4)
        local holder = ffi.new<int32[1]>()
        local value: any = nil
        -- A pointer is not indexable in checked code, so the bytes are copied into a
        -- one-element array and read from there. Same instruction, checked shape.
        unsafe do
            ffi.copy(holder, raw, 4)
            value = holder[0]
        end

        return nupp.math.i32.wrap(value as integer)
    end

    --- Reads one unsigned 64-bit integer in the machine's byte order.
    --- @param self this reader
    --- @return the value, as `uint64` cdata
    --- @raises when fewer than 8 bytes are left
    function readUint64(self): uint64
        local raw = taken(self, 8)
        local holder = ffi.new<uint64[1]>()
        local value: any = nil
        -- A pointer is not indexable in checked code, so the bytes are copied into a
        -- one-element array and read from there. Same instruction, checked shape.
        unsafe do
            ffi.copy(holder, raw, 8)
            value = holder[0]
        end

        return value as uint64
    end

    --- Reads one signed 64-bit integer in the machine's byte order.
    --- @param self this reader
    --- @return the value, as `int64` cdata
    --- @raises when fewer than 8 bytes are left
    function readInt64(self): int64
        local raw = taken(self, 8)
        local holder = ffi.new<int64[1]>()
        local value: any = nil
        -- A pointer is not indexable in checked code, so the bytes are copied into a
        -- one-element array and read from there. Same instruction, checked shape.
        unsafe do
            ffi.copy(holder, raw, 8)
            value = holder[0]
        end

        return value as int64
    end

    --- Reads one 32-bit float in the machine's byte order.
    --- @param self this reader
    --- @return the value, widened to a Lua number
    --- @raises when fewer than 4 bytes are left
    function readFloat32(self): float
        local raw = taken(self, 4)
        local holder = ffi.new<float[1]>()
        local value: any = nil
        -- A pointer is not indexable in checked code, so the bytes are copied into a
        -- one-element array and read from there. Same instruction, checked shape.
        unsafe do
            ffi.copy(holder, raw, 4)
            value = holder[0]
        end

        return nupp.math.f32.narrow(value as number)
    end

    --- Reads one 64-bit float in the machine's byte order.
    --- @param self this reader
    --- @return the value
    --- @raises when fewer than 8 bytes are left
    function readFloat64(self): number
        local raw = taken(self, 8)
        local holder = ffi.new<number[1]>()
        local value: any = nil
        -- A pointer is not indexable in checked code, so the bytes are copied into a
        -- one-element array and read from there. Same instruction, checked shape.
        unsafe do
            ffi.copy(holder, raw, 8)
            value = holder[0]
        end

        return value as number
    end

    --- Closes the reader, and the reader it was built over when there was one.
    --- @param self this reader, spent by the call
    --- @return whether the underlying source closed cleanly
    --- @return why it did not, when it did not
    function close(takes self): (boolean, string?)
        local state = unsafe release self
        local reader = state._reader
        state._reader = nil
        state._queue = nil
        state._pending = ""
        state._closed = true
        if reader then
            reader:close()
        end

        return true, nil
    end
end

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Scalars
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

--- The storage behind a scalar reader.
local record Scalars is ScalarReader
    _pending: string
    _reader: any
    _queue: any
    _closed: boolean
end

--- Writes fixed-width numbers into a destination.
--- @export
export interface ScalarWriter
    _buffer: any
    _writer: any
    _ownsBuffer: boolean
    _closed: boolean

    --- Releases it as an ownership terminal.
    ---
    --- Repeated calls are safe. Declared rather than written inline: a cleanup
    --- contract is `nosuspend`, and an inline method cannot carry one.
    --- @param self this owner, spent by the call
    drop: nosuspend function(takes self: ScalarWriter): nil

    --- Writes bytes as they are.
    --- @param self this writer
    --- @param bytes the bytes to write
    --- @return this writer
    --- @raises when bytes is not a string
    function writeBytes(self, bytes: string): ScalarWriter
        if type(bytes) ~= "string" then
            error("nupp: io.ScalarWriter bytes must be a string", 2)
        end

        return put(self, bytes)
    end

    --- Writes one unsigned 8-bit integer.
    --- @param self this writer
    --- @param value the number to write
    --- @return this writer
    function writeUint8(self, value: uint32): ScalarWriter
        local holder = ffi.new<uint8[1]>()
        unsafe do
            holder[0] = value
        end

        return put(self, ffi.string(holder, 1))
    end

    --- Writes one signed 8-bit integer.
    --- @param self this writer
    --- @param value the number to write
    --- @return this writer
    function writeInt8(self, value: int32): ScalarWriter
        local holder = ffi.new<int8[1]>()
        unsafe do
            holder[0] = value
        end

        return put(self, ffi.string(holder, 1))
    end

    --- Writes one unsigned 16-bit integer in the machine's byte order.
    --- @param self this writer
    --- @param value the number to write
    --- @return this writer
    function writeUint16(self, value: uint32): ScalarWriter
        local holder = ffi.new<uint16[1]>()
        unsafe do
            holder[0] = value
        end

        return put(self, ffi.string(holder, 2))
    end

    --- Writes one signed 16-bit integer in the machine's byte order.
    --- @param self this writer
    --- @param value the number to write
    --- @return this writer
    function writeInt16(self, value: int32): ScalarWriter
        local holder = ffi.new<int16[1]>()
        unsafe do
            holder[0] = value
        end

        return put(self, ffi.string(holder, 2))
    end

    --- Writes one unsigned 32-bit integer in the machine's byte order.
    --- @param self this writer
    --- @param value the number to write
    --- @return this writer
    function writeUint32(self, value: uint32): ScalarWriter
        local holder = ffi.new<uint32[1]>()
        unsafe do
            holder[0] = value
        end

        return put(self, ffi.string(holder, 4))
    end

    --- Writes one signed 32-bit integer in the machine's byte order.
    --- @param self this writer
    --- @param value the number to write
    --- @return this writer
    function writeInt32(self, value: int32): ScalarWriter
        local holder = ffi.new<int32[1]>()
        unsafe do
            holder[0] = value
        end

        return put(self, ffi.string(holder, 4))
    end

    --- Writes one unsigned 64-bit integer in the machine's byte order.
    --- @param self this writer
    --- @param value the number to write
    --- @return this writer
    function writeUint64(self, value: uint64): ScalarWriter
        local holder = ffi.new<uint64[1]>()
        unsafe do
            holder[0] = value
        end

        return put(self, ffi.string(holder, 8))
    end

    --- Writes one signed 64-bit integer in the machine's byte order.
    --- @param self this writer
    --- @param value the number to write
    --- @return this writer
    function writeInt64(self, value: int64): ScalarWriter
        local holder = ffi.new<int64[1]>()
        unsafe do
            holder[0] = value
        end

        return put(self, ffi.string(holder, 8))
    end

    --- Writes one 32-bit float in the machine's byte order.
    --- @param self this writer
    --- @param value the number to write
    --- @return this writer
    function writeFloat32(self, value: float): ScalarWriter
        local holder = ffi.new<float[1]>()
        unsafe do
            holder[0] = value
        end

        return put(self, ffi.string(holder, 4))
    end

    --- Writes one 64-bit float in the machine's byte order.
    --- @param self this writer
    --- @param value the number to write
    --- @return this writer
    function writeFloat64(self, value: number): ScalarWriter
        local holder = ffi.new<number[1]>()
        unsafe do
            holder[0] = value
        end

        return put(self, ffi.string(holder, 8))
    end

    --- The buffer this writer fills, when it has one of its own.
    --- @param self this writer
    --- @return the buffer, or nil when writing through a writer
    function buffer(borrows self): Buffer? borrows (self)
        return self._buffer as Buffer?
    end

    --- Flushes the destination, when there is one to flush.
    --- @param self this writer
    --- @return whether it flushed
    --- @return why it did not, when it did not
    function flush(self): (boolean, string?)
        if self._closed then
            return false, "the writer is closed"
        end
        if self._writer then
            return self._writer:flush()
        end

        return true, nil
    end

    --- Closes the writer, and the writer it was built over when there was one.
    --- @param self this writer, spent by the call
    --- @return whether the underlying destination closed cleanly
    --- @return why it did not, when it did not
    function close(takes self): (boolean, string?)
        local state = unsafe release self
        local writer = state._writer
        local buffer = state._buffer
        local ownsBuffer = state._ownsBuffer
        state._writer = nil
        state._buffer = nil
        state._closed = true
        if writer then
            writer:close()
            return true, nil
        end
        if ownsBuffer and buffer then
            buffer:drop()
        end

        return true, nil
    end
end

--- The storage behind a scalar writer.
local record ScalarSink is ScalarWriter
    _buffer: any
    _writer: any
    _ownsBuffer: boolean
    _closed: boolean
end

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Dropping
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

function Snapshot.drop(takes self: Snapshot): nil
    local state = unsafe release self
    state._closed = true
    state._bytes = ""
end

function HeapBuffer.drop(takes self: HeapBuffer): nil
    local state = unsafe release self
    state._closed = true
    state._length = 0
    state._capacity = 0
end

function WriteLease.drop(takes self: WriteLease): nil
    nosuspend do
        local _raw = unsafe release self
    end
end

function BufferWriter.drop(takes self: BufferWriter): nil
    local state = unsafe release self
    state._closed = true
end

function StringReader.drop(takes self: StringReader): nil
    local state = unsafe release self
    state._closed = true
    state._bytes = ""
end

function QueueReader.drop(takes self: QueueReader): nil
    local state = unsafe release self
    state._closed = true
    state._source = nil
end

function Scalars.drop(takes self: Scalars): nil
    local state = unsafe release self
    local reader = state._reader
    state._closed = true
    state._pending = ""
    state._queue = nil
    state._reader = nil
    if reader then
        reader:close()
    end
end

function ScalarSink.drop(takes self: ScalarSink): nil
    local state = unsafe release self
    local writer = state._writer
    local buffer = state._buffer
    local ownsBuffer = state._ownsBuffer
    state._closed = true
    state._writer = nil
    state._buffer = nil
    if writer then
        writer:close()
    elseif ownsBuffer and buffer then
        buffer:drop()
    end
end

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Shared implementations
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Doubling, with a floor, so appending one byte at a time stays linear overall.
reserve = function(borrows self: Buffer, minimum: integer): nil
    local state = self as any
    if minimum <= state._capacity then
        return
    end
    local capacity = state._capacity * 2
    if capacity < minimum then
        capacity = minimum
    end
    if capacity < SMALLEST then
        capacity = SMALLEST
    end
    local data = ffi.new<uint8[?]>(capacity)
    if state._length > 0 then
        ffi.copy(data, state._data, state._length)
    end
    state._data = data
    state._capacity = capacity
end

bytesAt = function(borrows self: Buffer, offset: integer, count: integer): string
    if count == 0 then
        return ""
    end

    return ffi.string((self as any)._data + offset, count)
end

newView = function(bytes: string): affine(ByteView, destroyOwner)
    return new Snapshot(_bytes = bytes, _closed = false)
end

newReaderOver = function(bytes: string): Reader
    return new StringReader(_bytes = bytes, _at = 1, _closed = false)
end

newWriterOver = function(borrows buffer: Buffer): affine(Writer) borrows (buffer)
    return new BufferWriter(anchor = buffer, _buffer = buffer, _at = 0, _closed = false)
end

-- Pulls from whichever source this reader was built over until `need` bytes are
-- pending or the source stops answering. A reader that reports a failure stops the
-- fill; the caller decides whether that is fatal.
fill = function(self: ScalarReader, need: integer): (integer, string?)
    local state = self as any
    local failure: string? = nil
    if state._reader then
        while #(state._pending as string) < need do
            local chunk, reason = state._reader:read(need - #(state._pending as string))
            if chunk == nil then
                failure = reason
                break
            elseif chunk == "" then
                break
            end
            state._pending = (state._pending as string) .. (chunk as string)
        end
    elseif state._queue then
        local want = need - #(state._pending as string)
        local have = want > 0 and #state._queue or 0
        if have > 0 then
            state._pending = (state._pending as string) .. state._queue:get(math.min(want, have))
        end
    end

    return #(state._pending as string), failure
end

--- @raises when the reader is closed, its source failed, or it is short
taken = function(self: ScalarReader, need: integer): string
    local state = self as any
    if state._closed then
        error("nupp: io.ScalarReader is closed", 3)
    end
    local have, failure = fill(self, need)
    if failure then
        error("nupp: io.ScalarReader source failed: " .. tostring(failure), 3)
    end
    if have < need then
        error(("nupp: io.ScalarReader needs %d bytes, has %d"):format(need, have), 3)
    end
    local out = (state._pending as string):sub(1, need)
    state._pending = (state._pending as string):sub(need + 1)

    return out
end

--- @raises when the writer is closed, or its destination failed
put = function(self: ScalarWriter, bytes: string): ScalarWriter
    local state = self as any
    if state._closed then
        error("nupp: io.ScalarWriter is closed", 3)
    end
    if state._buffer then
        local buffer = state._buffer as Buffer
        buffer:setString(bytes, buffer:length())

        return self
    end
    local ok, reason = state._writer:write(bytes)
    if not ok then
        error("nupp: io.ScalarWriter destination failed: " .. tostring(reason), 3)
    end

    return self
end

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Opening
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

local function queueLike(value: any): boolean
    local kind = type(value)
    if kind == "table" then
        return value.get ~= nil
    elseif kind == "userdata" or kind == "cdata" then
        -- Reading a field off foreign data can raise, so asking is done under pcall.
        local ok, getter = pcall(function(): any
            return value.get
        end)

        return ok and getter ~= nil
    else
        return false
    end
end

--- Creates a buffer, optionally over starting bytes or a starting capacity.
---
--- The buffer is an owner, so it closes at its lexical boundary unless it is
--- transferred out of one. A number is a capacity to allocate rather than
--- content, and the buffer is still empty afterwards.
---
--- ```nupp
--- const io = require("nupp.io")
---
--- do
---     local buffer = io.newBuffer("hello")
---     assert(buffer:length() == 5)
---
---     local sized = io.newBuffer(4096)
---     assert(sized:length() == 0 and sized:capacity() == 4096)
--- end
--- ```
---
--- @param initial the starting bytes, a starting capacity, or nothing
--- @return the buffer, owned by the caller
--- @raises when initial is neither bytes nor a capacity
--- @export
export function newBuffer(initial: integer | string?): affine(Buffer, destroyOwner)
    if initial ~= nil and type(initial) ~= "number" and type(initial) ~= "string" then
        error("nupp: io.newBuffer initial value must be bytes or a capacity", 2)
    end
    local bytes = type(initial) == "string" and initial as string or ""
    local capacity = type(initial) == "number" and integer_(initial, "io.newBuffer capacity", 2) or #bytes
    local self = new HeapBuffer(
        _data = ffi.new<uint8[?]>(math.max(capacity, 1)),
        _length = 0,
        _capacity = capacity,
        _closed = false
    )
    if #bytes > 0 then
        local state = self as any
        ffi.copy(state._data, bytes, #bytes)
        state._length = #bytes
    end

    return self
end

--- Opens a reader over bytes held as a string.
---
--- The reader is a `Reader` like any other, so a parser written against the
--- contract reads a string here and a file or a response body elsewhere without
--- changing:
---
--- ```nupp
--- local reader = io.newStringReader("hello")
--- assert(reader:read(5) == "hello")
--- assert(reader:read(1) == "")
--- ```
---
--- @param text the bytes to read
--- @return the reader, owned by the caller
--- @raises when text is not a string
--- @export
export function newStringReader(text: string): Reader
    if type(text) ~= "string" then
        error("nupp: io.newStringReader needs a string", 2)
    end

    return newReaderOver(text)
end

--- Opens a reader over a byte queue, which it does not own.
---
--- Closing this reader leaves the queue behind it open, because the queue was
--- never transferred. A process stream and an HTTP response body are both byte
--- queues without naming this module.
---
--- @param source the queue to read from
--- @return the reader, owned by the caller
--- @raises when source is not a byte queue
--- @export
export function newQueueReader(source: ByteQueue): Reader
    if not queueLike(source) then
        error("nupp: io.newQueueReader needs a byte queue", 2)
    end

    return new QueueReader(_source = source, _closed = false)
end

--- A reader taken apart into lines.
---
--- Reading a line means reading past its end, so something has to hold what came
--- after it. That is what this owns, and it is why a line reader takes its
--- source rather than borrowing one: two things reading the same bytes would
--- each see half of them.
--- @export
export sealed interface Lines is nupp.Closeable
    drop: nosuspend function(takes self: Lines): nil

    --- Reads the next line, without its terminator.
    ---
    --- Both `\n` and `\r\n` end a line. Bytes left after the last terminator are
    --- a line, because a stream that ended without one still said something.
    --- @param self this line reader
    --- @return the line, or nil at the end
    --- @return why it could not read, when unsuccessful
    read: function(self: Lines, ...: integer?): (string?, string?)

    --- Whether this reader has been released.
    isReleased: function(self: Lines): boolean

    --- Releases this reader and the source it took.
    close: function(takes self: Lines): nil
end

local record LineReader is Lines
    private _source: Reader
    private _held: string
    private _at: integer
    private _limit: integer
    private _closed: boolean
    private _ended: boolean

    drop: nosuspend function(takes self: LineReader): nil

    function isReleased(self): boolean
        return self._closed
    end

    function read(self, ...: integer?): (string?, string?)
        if self._closed then
            return nil, "the line reader is closed"
        end
        while true do
            const found = self._held:find("\n", self._at + 1, true)
            if found ~= nil then
                local line = self._held:sub(self._at + 1, found - 1)
                if line:sub(-1) == "\r" then
                    line = line:sub(1, -2)
                end
                -- Measured against the line, not against the bytes it arrived
                -- in. Checked here rather than only before asking for more,
                -- because a terminator in the same chunk as an overlong line
                -- would otherwise walk past the bound; and after the carriage
                -- return has gone, because neither terminator byte belongs to
                -- the line and counting one against the limit would refuse a
                -- line of exactly the length that was allowed.
                if #line > self._limit then
                    return nil, "a line was longer than the limit"
                end
                self._at = found
                if self._at >= #self._held then
                    self._held = ""
                    self._at = 0
                end

                return line
            end
            -- No terminator yet. A line that never ends is not a line, and
            -- growing until it does is how a peer takes the process down, so
            -- the bound is checked before asking for more rather than after.
            if #self._held - self._at > self._limit then
                return nil, "a line was longer than the limit"
            end
            if self._ended then
                if self._at >= #self._held then
                    return nil
                end
                const rest = self._held:sub(self._at + 1)
                self._held = ""
                self._at = 0

                return rest
            end
            const chunk, why = self._source:read(DEFAULT_READ as integer)
            if chunk == nil then
                return nil, why
            end
            if #chunk == 0 then
                self._ended = true
            else
                if self._at > 0 then
                    self._held = self._held:sub(self._at + 1)
                    self._at = 0
                end
                self._held = self._held .. chunk
            end
        end
    end

    function close(takes self): nil
        if self._closed then
            return
        end
        self._closed = true
        self._source:close()
    end
end

function LineReader.drop(takes self: LineReader): nil
    self:close()
end

--- Takes a reader apart into lines.
---
--- The source is taken rather than borrowed, and closing the result closes it:
--- a line reader holds the bytes it read past a terminator, so a second reader
--- over the same source would be reading a stream with holes in it.
--- @param source the reader to take
--- @param limit the most bytes one line may hold, or 65536
--- @return the line reader, owned by the caller
--- @raises when limit is not a positive integer
--- @export
export function newLines(takes source: Reader, limit: integer?): affine(Lines)
    const bound = limit or 65536
    if bound < 1 or math.floor(bound) ~= bound then
        error("nupp: io.newLines limit must be a positive integer", 2)
    end

    return new LineReader(_source = source, _held = "", _at = 0, _limit = bound, _closed = false, _ended = false)
end

-- The two scalar factories are declared below rather than written as exports,
-- because each accepts its sources under different ownership: a snapshot or buffer is
-- borrowed, a reader is taken, and a string or queue carries no obligation at all.
-- One parameter cannot say three things, so the surface is an intersection of three
-- signatures over one body.
local function openScalarReader(source: any): ScalarReader
    local self = new Scalars(_pending = "", _reader = nil, _queue = nil, _closed = false)
    local state = self as any
    local kind = type(source)
    if kind == "string" then
        state._pending = source
    elseif kind == "table" and source.read ~= nil then
        state._reader = source
    elseif kind == "table" and source.getString ~= nil then
        state._pending = source:getString()
    elseif queueLike(source) then
        state._queue = source
    else
        error("nupp: io.newScalarReader needs bytes, a snapshot, a buffer, a reader or a byte queue", 2)
    end

    return self
end

local function openScalarWriter(destination: any): ScalarWriter
    local self = new ScalarSink(_buffer = nil, _writer = nil, _ownsBuffer = false, _closed = false)
    local state = self as any
    if destination == nil then
        -- The scalar writer owns this buffer. `buffer()` only borrows it, and scalar
        -- teardown discharges the buffer obligation with the writer's own.
        state._buffer = unsafe release newBuffer(nil)
        state._ownsBuffer = true
    elseif type(destination) == "table" and destination.write ~= nil then
        state._writer = destination
    elseif type(destination) == "table" and destination.setString ~= nil then
        state._buffer = destination
    else
        error("nupp: io.newScalarWriter needs a buffer, a writer, or nothing", 2)
    end

    return self
end

--- Opens a cursor for sized integer and float reads.
---
--- A string, snapshot or buffer is read from a copy of its bytes taken here, and
--- reports its `remaining` count. A reader or a queue is consumed in place: the reader
--- answers no count, the queue answers what it still holds.
---
--- What the source is decides what happens to it. A snapshot or a buffer is
--- borrowed and outlives the cursor; a reader is taken, and closing the cursor
--- closes it; a string or a queue carries no obligation either way.
---
--- ```nupp
--- do
---     local cursor = io.newScalarReader(header)
---     local magic = cursor:readUint32()
---     local count = cursor:readUint16()
--- end
--- ```
---
--- @param source the bytes, snapshot, buffer, reader or queue to read
--- @return the new reader, owned by the caller
--- @raises when source is none of those
--- @export
export const newScalarReader: function(source: string | ByteQueue): affine(ScalarReader, destroyOwner)
    & function(borrows source: ByteView | Buffer): affine(ScalarReader, destroyOwner)
    & function(takes source: Reader): affine(ScalarReader, destroyOwner) = openScalarReader as any

--- Opens a cursor for sized integer and float writes.
---
--- A buffer is appended to, keeping what it already holds. A writer receives the bytes
--- as they are written. Omitting the destination starts a fresh buffer, which
--- `ScalarWriter:buffer` hands back.
---
--- ```nupp
--- do
---     local cursor = io.newScalarWriter()
---     cursor:writeUint32(0x4d495355):writeUint16(2)
---     local bytes = cursor:buffer()
---     assert(bytes ~= nil and bytes:getString() ~= "")
--- end
--- ```
---
--- @param destination the buffer or writer to append to, or nothing for a fresh buffer
--- @return the new writer, owned by the caller
--- @raises when destination is neither a buffer nor a writer
--- @export
export const newScalarWriter: function(): affine(ScalarWriter, destroyOwner)
    & function(borrows destination: Buffer): affine(ScalarWriter, destroyOwner) borrows (destination)
    & function(takes destination: Writer): affine(ScalarWriter, destroyOwner) = openScalarWriter as any
